# ===================================================================
#  LogOS kernel — K5b.2 probe: PREEMPTIVE tasks (safe-point yield-flag).
#
#  Two worker tasks each print their label REPS times, with a bounded compute
#  SPIN between prints, and NEVER call yield(). On the metal the K5a timer
#  (IRQ0 ~100 Hz) fires asynchronously; with -dK5B2 the timer ISR sets the
#  runtime's YIELD_PENDING flag, and rt_apply's safe point (checked on every
#  reduction, between reductions — never inside rt_gc/alloc) honours it and
#  context-switches. So the two NON-yielding workers interleave purely because
#  the timer preempts them — cooperative scheduling would keep A running to
#  completion before B ever starts.
#
#  OBSERVABLE:
#    - preemption working  -> A and B INTERLEAVE (e.g. A A B B A B ...): a B is
#      printed before A has finished all REPS, and vice-versa.
#    - preemption absent    -> strict blocks "A*REPS then B*REPS then done"
#      (each bounded worker still terminates and yields at the end, so the demo
#      never hangs — it just fails to interleave).
#
#  BOUNDED ALLOCATION (blocker-2 / the GC retention residual): the workers are
#  bounded (REPS * SPINCOUNT reductions total), so the tight-loop garbage the
#  conservative collector retains stays FAR below the 896 MiB task-stack floor
#  (task stacks at TASK_STACK_TOP=0x38000000 on metal; MAIN stack at 0x3F000000;
#  heap grows up from ~68 MiB). Tune SPINCOUNT so a worker spans >= one 10 ms
#  tick (enough to be preempted) without climbing the heap into the stacks.
#
#  METAL-ONLY: on Linux YIELD_PENDING is never set (no timer), so a worker would
#  run uninterrupted to completion — the interleave only appears on QEMU. Built
#  with -dK5_TIMER -dK5B2, run under QEMU -m 1024 (needs 0x3F000000 mapped).
# ===================================================================

glyph Z   = la f. (la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))(la x. f(la v. x(x)(v)))
glyph IF  = la c. la t. la f. c(t)(f)("!")
glyph SEQ = la a. la b. b

# A bounded busy loop (tail-recursive -> bounded stack via TCO; each iteration
# allocates one boxed int the 4 MiB periodic GC reclaims). SPINCOUNT (=2500) is the
# tuning knob: on the metal ~2500 iterations span ~30 ms — MORE than one 10 ms tick
# — so a tick reliably lands mid-spin and the safe point preempts the worker there.
# Small enough that REPS*SPINCOUNT garbage stays far below the 896 MiB task-stack
# floor. Wrapped in `la _.` so each reference re-runs the loop.
glyph SPIN = la _.
    (Z(la self. la i. IF(lt(i)(2500)) (la _. self(add(i)(1))) (la _. i)))(0)

# Worker: print lbl, spin, repeat REPS(=6) times. NO yield — preemption drives the
# switch. Bounded (~6 spins), so it finishes in only a handful of scheduler slices
# and terminates even with no timer (serial-block fallback).
glyph WORKER = la lbl. la _.
    (Z(la self. la i.
        IF(lt(i)(6))
            (la _. SEQ(print(lbl))(SEQ(SPIN("!"))(self(add(i)(1)))))
            (la _. lbl)))(0)

# MAIN: spawn both workers, then drain via a yield loop. DRAIN(=200) is MUCH larger
# than the ~12 scheduler slices the two 6-rep workers need, so MAIN never races them
# to "done": for the whole time the workers run, MAIN advances at most one drain step
# per round-robin round (a plain yield, no spin), so it is nowhere near 200 when they
# finish. Once both workers are dead MAIN alone burns the remaining (fast, no-spin)
# steps to 200, prints "done", and falls through to exit(0).
glyph MAIN =
    SEQ(spawn(WORKER("A")))
   (SEQ(spawn(WORKER("B")))
   (SEQ((Z(la self. la i.
            IF(lt(i)(200))
                (la _. SEQ(yield("!"))(self(add(i)(1))))
                (la _. "!")))(0))
   (print("done"))))
